func Bill(f machine.Frame[Order]) Receipt {
  // ONE UPDATE, NOT A LOAD THEN A SAVE, for the reason the e2e fixture states:
  // the runtime dispatches every datum concurrently, so a read-modify-write
  // split across two calls loses increments. Update returns the NEW value.
  count, err := f.Update(seen, func(n int) int { return n + 1 })
  if err != nil {
    panic(err)
  }
  f.Set(attempt, f.Get(attempt)+1)
  return Receipt{ID: strings.ToUpper(f.Value().ID), Seen: count}
}

flow orders
note """
The canonical shape: a source feeding one transform that touches both handle
kinds, then a sink. It carries a const, a param, an error handler at both the
flow and the node level, an `over` transport clause and a verbatim func.

BOTH HANDLES APPEAR IN BOTH CLAUSES ON PURPOSE. A write capability does not
imply a read one, and Bill reads-modifies-writes each handle: Update takes read
AND write on the cell, Get and Set take read AND write on the key. Declaring
either half alone panics with a CapabilityError on the first datum, which a
compile-only fixture cannot notice — so this flow is also RUN, by the criterion
that stages the checked-in golden and requires the sink's own token.
"""
import "strings"

const Retries int = 3
param Endpoint string = ":8080"

state {
  seen int
}
var attempt int

on error PageOnCall

source ingest Poll()
  over machine.Channel[Order](0)
transform charge Bill from ingest
  reads attempt, seen  writes attempt, seen
  on error NodeFailed
sink done Store from charge
